his ratification of the withdrawal  agreement as approved by the UK  government and endorsed by this  Parliament and also by the European  Council ten days ago the European  Council followed the request of the  extension of article 15 till the 22nd of  May on the condition that the House of  Commons approve withdrawal deal before  the 29th of March unfortunately that  didn't happen making reference to the  statement by the Prime Minister of the  UK last night I say that though we have  a few days left if the UK is in a  position of approving the withdrawal  deal with a viable majority by the 12th  of April then the UK in that case should  also the European Union would also  accept an extension until the 22nd of  May 12th of April is the final date for  possible approval if the House of  Commons does not adopt a stance before  that date no extension no short-term  extension will be possible after the  12th of April we run the risk of  jeopardizing the correct running of the  European elections and the correct  functioning of the European Union the  withdrawal agreement has always been a  compromise a balanced compromise which  has enabled both parties to personally  obtain what they wanted but not  everything that they wanted its with  that kind of compromise that the  European Union has been built from the  beginning it's that kind of compromise  which has enabled the European project  to move on and it's that kind of  compromise  that we need now  a great part of the debate in the House  of Commons has been about the future  relationship between the EU and the UK  the EU is prepared to add a certain  degree of flexibility to the political  declaration in order to open the door to  a close partnership between the EU and  the UK in the future we're open to a  whole panoply of different options it  could be a free-trade agreement it could  be a customs union or a European  Economic Area we've been open since the  beginning we see this in the political  declaration the EU is prepared to begin  discussions negotiations on our future  partnership as soon as the withdrawal  deal is signed even before the ink is  dry the Commission team is ready to go  my friend  Michele Barney our chief negotiator is  ready and I would like to see the same  level of preparation on the side of the  UK whether or not that happens depends  entirely on the UK European Council has  given all necessary time and space to  the UK to take its decision I believe a  No Deal on the 12th of April at midnight  has become a scenario which looks more  and more likely it's not what I want but  we have made sure that the EU is ready  to face up to that situation we've been  preparing since December 2017 we've  always known that the logic of article  50 makes no deal a default option we've  known for a long time what the balance  of powers is in the House of Commons the  Commission has  has published 91 notes on brexit  preparation 32 logistic proposals 3  communications we've been to each one of  the 27 member states to help them to  prepare we have organized a 72 seminars  with member states the measures that  member states and ourselves have taken  will attenuate the harshest impact of  the shock of a No Deal they offer true  protection they will guarantee that  citizens of the EU and the UK will  continue to live and work wherever they  reside now it will make sure that  aeroplanes will continue to be able to  take off and land we have adapted our  financial instruments in order to assist  the fishing sector we have determined  the mechanisms which will enable us to  continue our police cooperation and  we've taken all necessary measures to  attenuate the disruption in the  financial markets and the measures that  we have taken are temporary in nature  they are unilateral they will protect  the key interests of the Union at least  until the end of the year but the  disturbances suffered by citizens  businesses in almost all sectors will be  unavoidable the UK will be more affected  than the EU because a managed or  negotiated No Deal does not exist any  more so than a transition period for a  No Deal  whatever happens the UK will have to  respond to the three main questions of  the separation one citizen's rights they  must be respected and protected - the UK  will have to continue to honor its  financial commitments taken as  member-state and three a solution will  have to be found for the island of  ireland preserving peace and the single  market the UK must respect the spirit  and the letter of the Good Friday  Agreement no withdrawal agreement does  not mean no commitment and the three  questions that I've just mentioned will  not disappear overnight  they will constitute strict conditions  for rebuilding trust and launching  discussions on the future la next week  in the European Council we will hear  Teresa may the prime minister explaining  her intentions and we will decide what  to do the principles that will guide my  action are limpid we will work together  Member States and the European Union  will work together to avoid a no deal  situation until the last minute the only  ones that would benefit from a No Deal  are those that are against a world order  based on rules the only ones that will  be strengthened will be the populace and  nationalists  the only ones who will be delighted will  be those who wish to weaken the European  Union and of course the UK the EU will  not expel any member state together with  Michele we will do everything possible  to avoid a disorganized exit working  together with the governments of the 27  and the European Parliament encouraging  them to do the same including the UK is  the first time that I've read a speech  in this Parliament because I believe  that each and every word has its  importance thank you for listening
 

